title: 5. Structs and methods
nav: Learn
order: 500
template: docs
---
<p class="lede">A struct groups named values into one type. You give it fields, methods and a way to print itself. This chapter builds a few small structs and shows what copying, changing and printing them looks like.</p>

<h2 id="struct">Fields and values</h2>
<example name="book-05-point"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>struct Point { i64 x  i64 y }</code> declares a type with two fields, type first, one per line.</li>
  <li><code>Point { x: 3, y: 4 }</code> makes a value. Every field is named in the construction.</li>
  <li>A struct is a value. <code>var q = p</code> makes a copy, so changing <code>q.x</code> leaves <code>p</code> alone: the output shows <code>p.x is 3, q.x is 10</code>.</li>
  <li><code>==</code> compares all fields. <code>p == Point { x: 3, y: 4 }</code> is true.</li>
  <li><code>{p, as: .debug}</code> prints every field with its name, which is what you want while you are working.</li>
</ul>
<p>Leave out a field and the compiler names it:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2109:</span> `Point` needs a value for `y`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> missingfield.byn:6:9

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> let p = Point { x: 3 }
<span class="gut">    │</span>         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: set it: `Point { y: … }`</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2109</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check missingfield.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>missingfield.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A field can have a default (<code>i64 count = 0</code>, below), and then it may be left out.</p>

<h2 id="methods">Methods</h2>
<example name="book-05-methods"/>
<ul>
  <li>A method is a function declared inside the struct. <code>string describe() =&gt; $"{name}: {count}"</code> reads the fields directly.</li>
  <li>A field-like member with no parentheses, <code>bool isZero =&gt; count == 0</code>, is called without them: <code>hits.isZero</code>.</li>
  <li>A method is read-only by default. One that changes the value says so with <code>ref this</code> as its first parameter: <code>void add(ref this, i64 amount = 1)</code>. At the call it is plain: <code>hits.add()</code>. Other parameters work as in <a href="book-04-functions.html">chapter 4</a>: here <code>amount</code> has a default and is passed by name.</li>
  <li><code>static</code> declares a function on the type itself, called as <code>Counter.named("hits")</code>. Use it to build values.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you forget <code>ref this</code>, the compiler finds the line that changes the field:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2115:</span> `add` modifies `this` but is not declared `ref this`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> noreflhis.byn:5:9

<span class="gut">  5 │</span>         count += 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>         ^^^^^ `this` is modified here
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: declare it `ref this` as the first parameter</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 4 │</span>     void add(ref this) {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">              ++++++++</span>

<span class="err">warning BYN2311:</span> `c` is never reassigned; declare it with `let`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> noreflhis.byn:9:5

<span class="gut">  9 │</span> var c = Counter {}
<span class="gut">    │</span>     ^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: declare it with `let` to tell the reader the value never changes</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 9 │</span> let c = Counter {}
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add"> +++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 1 warning</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2115</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check noreflhis.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>noreflhis.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>It also warns that the <code>var</code> in the program is never reassigned. That is a side effect of the example: the fix to the method comes first.</p>
<p>A <code>ref this</code> method cannot be called on a <code>let</code>:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `c` is immutable
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> letref.byn:10:1

<span class="gut">  10 │</span> c.add()
<span class="gut">     │</span> ^ `add` modifies it; declared with `let` on line 9
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: declare it with `var` if it should change</span>
<span class="gut">     │  9 │</span> var c = Counter {}
<span class="gut">     │    │</span><span class="add"> +++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2104</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check letref.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>letref.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The label under <code>c</code> says why: <code>add</code> modifies it. The fix is <code>var</code>.</p>

<h2 id="with">Copy and update with <code>with</code></h2>
<example name="book-05-with"/>
<p><code>ada with { age: 37 }</code> makes a new <code>User</code> with the same fields except the ones you list. <code>ada</code> itself is unchanged, which is why this works on a <code>let</code>. Use it when you would rather build a new value than change one in place.</p>

<h2 id="format">Printing your own types</h2>
<p>A struct does not print by itself. Interpolating one is refused:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2225:</span> `Point` cannot be formatted
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> formatstruct.byn:7:10

<span class="gut">  7 │</span> print($"{p}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>          ^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: add `formatInto(ref StrBuf)` to `Point`, or use `{x, as: .debug}`</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2225</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check formatstruct.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>formatstruct.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The help gives both options. <code>{p, as: .debug}</code> prints the fields. For output users see, give the type a <code>formatInto</code> method:</p>
<example name="book-05-format"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>formatInto(ref StrBuf sb)</code> appends the text to a string buffer. Inside, you can interpolate again: <code>width: 2, fill: '0'</code> pads the cents to two digits with zeros.</li>
  <li>Once <code>Money</code> has it, <code>{tea.price}</code> prints <code>$4.50</code>.</li>
  <li>The debug form still shows the structure: <code>Line { item: "tea", price: Money { cents: 450 }, qty: 2 }</code>. It uses the field names, not your <code>formatInto</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="extensions">Methods on types you do not own</h2>
<p>You cannot add a method to <code>string</code> or <code>i64</code> yet. The specification has <code>extension</code> declarations for this, and the compiler refuses them:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2022:</span> `extension` declarations is not supported in v0.1
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> extension.byn:1:11

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> extension string {
<span class="gut">    │</span>           ^^^^^^

<span class="err">error BYN2003:</span> `isBlank` is not a member of `string`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> extension.byn:5:15

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> print($"{"  ".isBlank}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>               ^^^^^^^

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2022</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check extension.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>extension.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">extension string {
    bool isBlank =&gt; trimmed().isEmpty
}

"  ".isBlank   // true</code></pre>
