<h2 id="recap">What you have now</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Structs hold named fields. Values are copied; <code>==</code> compares fields.</li>
  <li>Methods read by default; <code>ref this</code> marks the ones that change the value, and they need a <code>var</code>.</li>
  <li><code>with</code> makes a changed copy.</li>
  <li><code>formatInto</code> controls printing; <code>as: .debug</code> prints fields.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<example name="book-05-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>It prints:</p>
<pre><code>1 2 3</code></pre>
  <p><code>b</code> is a copy of <code>a</code>, so changing it leaves <code>a.w</code> at 1. <code>c</code> is made from <code>b</code> with <code>w</code> replaced by 3, and <code>b</code> stays 2. If you answered <code>2 2 3</code> you treated <code>b</code> as another name for <code>a</code>.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-05-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>It prints:</p>
<pre><code>5
Tally { n: 5 }</code></pre>
  <p><code>add()</code> uses the default 1 and <code>add(amount: 4)</code> adds 4, so <code>n</code> is 5. The second line is the debug form. A line such as <code>Tally { n: 1 }</code> would mean only the first call took effect.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>A method does <code>count += 1</code> but its signature is <code>void add()</code>. What happens?</summary>
  <p>The compiler refuses (<code>BYN2115</code>) and points at the line that changes the field. The help adds <code>ref this</code>. It does not compile and then fail later, and the method does not quietly work on a copy.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>You write <code>let c = Counter {}</code> then <code>c.add()</code>, where <code>add</code> is a <code>ref this</code> method. Why is it an error?</summary>
  <p>A <code>let</code> value cannot change, and <code>add</code> changes it (<code>BYN2104</code>). Declare <code>var c</code>. The method is not wrong, and neither is the call; the declaration is what has to change.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Why does <code>print($"{p}")</code> fail for a struct <code>Point</code>?</summary>
  <p>The compiler does not guess how your type should look (<code>BYN2225</code>). Add <code>formatInto(ref StrBuf)</code> to the struct, or print with <code>{p, as: .debug}</code>.</p>
</details>

